# Core Constraints

## NEVER DO
- NEVER split existing manager files (GridManager.cs, PlayerManager.cs, WaveManager.cs, StageManager.cs, etc.) without explicit approval
- NEVER introduce new singleton patterns without approval
- NEVER modify `Assets/scripts/Utils/Enumerations.cs` without approval
- NEVER change Unity lifecycle patterns (Awake, Start, Update sequences)
- NEVER create new documentation files without approval
- NEVER include technical implementation details in Project Doc files (see documentation-standards.mdc)
- NEVER use FindObjectOfType<>() in Update() or repeating methods
- NEVER create unbounded loops without exit conditions
- NEVER allocate large arrays in frequently called methods
- NEVER use reflection in gameplay code
- NEVER commit partial method implementations
- NEVER leave incomplete control structures (if/for/while)
- NEVER remove debug logging from critical operations
- NEVER bypass validation checks

## ALWAYS DO
- ALWAYS execute tasks one at a time via Shrimp (never parallel execution)
- ALWAYS run build validation before marking task complete
- ALWAYS check file size limits before modifications:
  - Core Components: 750 lines max
  - Manager Classes: 600 lines max
  - Utility Classes: 300 lines max
  - Data Classes: 300 lines max
  - Interfaces: 200 lines max
- ALWAYS score complexity after planning (≥7 requires approval)
- ALWAYS validate manager references in Start()
- ALWAYS use the standard debug pattern
- ALWAYS follow the manager reference pattern (cache in Start(), not Update())
- ALWAYS handle null references gracefully
- ALWAYS follow documentation standards: Project Doc = design focus, Technical Doc = implementation focus (see documentation-standards.mdc)
